From e83ccfbd8082bfd8c138c2d237f9133fb1c3652b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:05:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] goal loop: delegate the wait to a zero-model-call watcher
 instead of parking the goal
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A goal that depends on one long-running job parked ITSELF on that job. The
pane, verbatim:

  Goal parked -- waiting on session proc_0a1b2c3d4e5f: The goal is not
  complete because the final v8 cut and the ranking pass depend on the running
  index_pass2 job, which the agent is waiting on.
  Loop: next in 30m.

The record behind it was active at turn 1 of 400, parked for 1,131 s, with
zero subgoals, on a turn whose own report named three other pieces of work in
progress. 399 turns unspent.  [measured]

Three compounding defects:

 1. An un-itemised goal cannot escalate through DEFER. all_items_blocked()
    returns False with no itemised criteria by design, so the judge is left
    with a binary: continue, or park everything. Nothing enumerates the work
    it is about to abandon.
 2. The whole-goal barrier had no age ceiling, while per-ITEM barriers have
    carried one since they existed -- on the stated reasoning that a process
    which never exits must not permanently remove an item from the work set.
    The barrier that removes the WHOLE GOAL had none.
 3. stalled_active_goals() skipped every parked goal, because "its barrier
    will release it". The release is LAZY, inside is_waiting(), which runs
    only from the post-turn hook -- and a parked goal fires no turns. A park
    had no backstop at all.

SOMEONE ELSE OWNS THE WAIT. A new module dispatches a watcher on the existing
async-delegation rail, in the single-unit form, so there is no parent agent
and therefore no CLI edit: all three surfaces already honour should_continue
and continuation_prompt.

A watcher makes ZERO model calls. It re-tests the barrier's own release
predicate and reports a bounded outcome, so there is no context, no toolset
and no stream for the broker's throttle or stream cap to act on, and nothing
to pay the measured contention tax with (2,622 wasted prefill tokens idle,
114,385 against five in-flight peers).  [measured] Its runner enters the
delegated-child context, so any incidental call is NORMAL priority while the
judge stays on the control-plane lane. progress_fn is deliberately NOT passed:
the stale-progress monitor kills a delegation on a frozen progress token, and
a watcher's token is frozen by design. It reads list_sessions() and never
poll()/read_log(), which would mark the session consumed and suppress the
registry's own completion turn.

The report re-enters the session through the completion queue, which forges a
fresh turn, which re-judges -- so a CORRECT park stops being a dead end too.

DECIDING "IS THERE OTHER WORK" HONESTLY. On a wait verdict, one small
auxiliary call asks what else is advanceable. Asking a model "anything else to
do?" reliably produces something, and busy-work is worse than the park, so the
guard is a CODE filter and not a prompt instruction: every proposed item must
carry a verbatim quote, and the quote is checked to really appear in the goal,
its criteria, or the agent's own report. The work has to already be written
down by the user or by the agent itself. A second filter drops the wait
restated as work. Every failure direction -- no client, transport error, bad
JSON, an honest NONE, nothing verified -- is the park.

A verified probe converts the whole-goal park into an ITEM barrier, the same
mechanism defer uses, and the continuation names the items and forbids
polling.

Also here: a default barrier age ceiling enforced in both is_waiting() and
reconcile_barriers(); barrier_past_ceiling() / barrier_looks_dead(), imported
by the supervisor rather than reimplemented; a waiting-session pid kept as a
liveness HINT and never as a release condition, because the process registry
persists nothing and a foreign process reads an unknown session as "not
waiting"; and the supervisor's third stall class, stale_barrier_goals().

Unchanged on purpose: a park with nothing advanceable still parks, and still
gets a watcher; the all-items-blocked escalation is not re-probed, because a
deterministic count outranks a model opinion; an explicit human `/goal wait`
is marked probed at the source, since a human's park is an instruction; a
`blocked` verdict still pauses for a human and is never auto-resumed; old
state rows load unchanged.

Squashed with two follow-ups:

  * the watcher had no routable session key, so its report would have been
    queued forever on exactly the surface it was written for. The CLI drains
    background completions through a POSITIVE-ownership filter that fails
    closed on an empty key, and the ambient resolver returns empty on the CLI
    (the contextvar is only bound during gateway/TUI turns). The manager's own
    durable session id IS that key; fall back to it, and refuse to dispatch
    at all when neither resolves, rather than promise a report nobody can
    claim.
  * a supervisor test parametrized its barrier rows with time.time(), which
    pytest evaluates at COLLECTION. In a 62-minute single-process run the test
    ran about an hour after collection, every timestamp was past the new
    1-hour barrier ceiling, and three "held park" cases read as stale barriers
    -- three failures that do not reproduce in isolation. The ceiling is
    right; the fixture was a clock captured at the wrong moment. Parametrize
    on the barrier KIND and derive the timestamps inside the test. Verified by
    running it with the wall clock shifted +2 h after collection, and by an AST
    audit that no decorator-level or module-level time.time() remains in
    either goal test file.

Requires patches 0007 and 0009.

Squashed from fork commits e83ccfb, 952f227, 7493255.

BASE: the tree left by patch 0010 (see BASELINE.md).

PUBLISHED FORM. The park this is built from is reproduced verbatim in the
tests, and it named an internal campaign and a session id. Those strings are
load-bearing for the quote-verification filter -- the test asserts a quote
really appears in the goal text -- so they are SUBSTITUTED rather than
paraphrased: the same structure and the same quoted spans, invented subject
matter. Two session ids are replaced throughout. A third replacement lands
on a CONTEXT line, which is normally forbidden -- that line's pre-image is a
line patch 0007 ADDS, so the published series is its base and the line has
to match what 0007 wrote. It is the only context line substituted anywhere
in the series; NOTICE says so.

